    pr_ref:  # e26d
    description: "MEASURED 2026-09-17 at 1aa5b46b1b, two-arm survey of\n~/whole_bunch_of_repos/aardvark-dns (16 .rs). Split out of WI-jivim, whose\nfiled core (origin_run_id violations) is fixed; this is what remained and it\nis larger.\n\n                       arm A (default)   arm B (backend on)\n    nodes                      581             1255\n    validation violations        0                2\n\nTHE TWO VIOLATIONS:\n\n  stable_id collision detected: 498/1255 Symbols (39.7% of all Symbols)\n  share an id. Top groups: sha256:114a2c4238480125 (10 symbols, e.g. \"1\");\n  sha256:2576637a38bf73b0 (10 symbols, e.g. \"3\")\n\n  Degenerate Symbol.fingerprint value(s): hgfp2:f7af508bf396c9bf (15 symbols,\n  15 distinct names, e.g. \"102\", \"17\", \"31\", \"38\", \"4\")\n\nTHE CAUSE. scip/index.py:158-159 mints a Symbol for every SCIP `local <id>`\nbinding and uses THE LOCAL ID AS THE NAME:\n\n    if scip_sym.is_local:\n        return scip_sym.local_id, \"local\"\n\nA SCIP local id is an index into the document, not an identifier: the names\nare literally \"1\", \"3\", \"17\", \"102\". 500 of the 669 scip nodes on this crate\nare locals -- 75% of the backend's node output and 40% of the whole artifact.\nNames that carry no lexical content collapse ADR-0014's stable_id inputs\n(name + qualified_name + path + kind), so they collide en masse, and the same\ndegeneracy hits Symbol.fingerprint.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN THE COUNT SUGGESTS. stable_id is the identity used\nfor cross-run tracking. A 39.7% collision rate means two-fifths of the\nartifact cannot be followed between runs, and the collision is SILENT to every\nconsumer that joins on it -- it reads as one symbol with many definitions\nrather than as an error. Note the precedent: INV-bazij re-designed stable_id\nprecisely because 155 zero-param bash functions in one file shared an id at a\n60% collision rate. This is the same failure mode, reached by a different\nroute, at 39.7%.\n\nTHE QUESTION THIS NEEDS, AND IT IS NOT MINE TO ANSWER. Should the SCIP\nimporter mint Symbols for function-local bindings at all? A behavior map is a\ngraph of things other things can refer to across files; a local binding is by\ndefinition not one. Dropping them would remove 75% of the backend's node\noutput and all of this defect -- but that is a RECALL-LOSS direction and a\ndesign decision about what the SCIP arm is for, so it is filed rather than\nperformed.\n\nThree shapes, if the answer is \"keep them\":\n  (a) qualify the name with the enclosing symbol, so \"1\" becomes\n      \"load_config::1\" and the stable_id inputs stop being degenerate;\n  (b) give locals a distinct id scheme that does not pretend to be a\n      cross-run identity, since a local has none;\n  (c) keep the nodes but exclude them from the stable_id / fingerprint\n      invariants, which is the weakest option -- it silences the detector\n      rather than fixing the identity.\n\n(a) is the smallest honest change and does not lose recall.\n\nREPRO: HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 hypergumbo survey <rust repo>, then read\nvalidation_report.violations. arm A for the control."  # e26d
    fields: {}  # e26d
- op: discuss  # eb69
  at: '2026-09-18T08:40:13Z'  # eb69
  by: agent  # eb69
  actor: jgstern_agent  # eb69
  clock: 2  # eb69
  nonce: eb69  # eb69
  message: "RE-POINTED 2026-09-18. TWO CLAIMS IN THIS ROW'S DESCRIPTION ARE WRONG, AND THE\nFIX IT RECOMMENDS WOULD NOT WORK. Re-pointing rather than striking; the 39.7%\ncollision measurement itself stands and reproduces at d6174d5df9.\n\n=== CORRECTION 1: THE STATED CAUSE IS WRONG ===\n\nThe description says: \"Names that carry no lexical content collapse ADR-0014's\nstable_id inputs (name + qualified_name + path + kind), so they collide en\nmasse.\"\n\nThe SCIP importer does not use those inputs. scip/index.py:251-252:\n\n    # INV-hunup: canonical sha256 stable_id. The SCIP moniker\n    # (sym_info.symbol) is a globally-stable identity, ...\n    stable_id=_short_sha256(sym_info.symbol),\n\nThe stable_id is sha256 of the RAW MONIKER. The name is never read. Verified\nagainst the live validator output:\n\n    sha256(\"local 1\")[:16] = 114a2c4238480125  -> validator group sha256:114a2c4238480125 (10 symbols, e.g. \"1\")\n    sha256(\"local 3\")[:16] = 2576637a38bf73b0  -> validator group sha256:2576637a38bf73b0 (10 symbols, e.g. \"3\")\n    sha256(\"local 2\")[:16] = c0504175a49d863b  -> validator group sha256:c0504175a49d863b (10 symbols, e.g. \"2\")\n\nThe real cause is that the comment's premise is FALSE FOR LOCALS: a SCIP moniker\nis NOT a globally-stable identity when it is `local <id>`. It is document-scoped,\nand `local 0` denotes a different binding in each of the ten files that define one.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ROW'S RECOMMENDED FIX: option (a) -- \"qualify the name with\nthe enclosing symbol, so '1' becomes 'load_config::1'\" -- was called \"the\nsmallest honest change\". It would change the display name and probably the\nfingerprint, and would leave the stable_id EXACTLY as it is, because the name is\nnot an input. Shipping it would look like a fix and do nothing to the 39.7%.\n\nThe correct fix is closer to this row's option (b): fold the document path into\nthe hashed key, so the moniker becomes per-document. **WI-titiz is the same\nfailure mode already fixed once in this codebase** -- a TOML dependency stable_id\nhashed only the package name and collapsed the same package across manifests;\nthe remedy was to fold the declaring manifest path into the hash\n(make_dependency_stable_id: \"a package declared in N manifests becomes N nodes,\neach keeping its own version constraint and provenance\"). Identical shape,\nidentical cure, already precedented.\n\n=== CORRECTION 2: A DETAIL I ASSERTED FROM AN 8-ROW SAMPLE ===\n\nI reported to the owner that all 455 local-pointing edges are sourced from a\nSINGLE node, `namespace 'crate'` in src/lib.rs. Enumerated rather than sampled:\nthere are NINE distinct sources, one per file's module namespace, and `crate`\naccounts for only 13 of the 455. The mechanism is systematic, not a freak\nartifact of one node. LIVE.md rule 3: let the grep enumerate, not your eyes.\n\n=== WHAT SPLIT OUT ===\n\nThe false-edge half of this finding is now its own row (the document-scoped-id\ninvariant, filed 2026-09-18): 329 of 455 local-pointing edges assert a\ncross-file reference to a function-local, which cannot exist -- 21.2% of every\nedge in the artifact. That is a correctness defect independent of identity, and\nit is the stronger reason to act.\n\nThe cross-backend stable_id parity question is also its own row (0 of 52\noverlapping symbols achieve parity; the test cannot fail). Relevant here because\nthis row's collisions are SCIP-internal while that row's are SCIP-vs-tree-sitter;\nboth trace to the same decision to hash a raw moniker rather than use the shared\nidentity factory.\n\n=== WHAT STILL STANDS ===\n\nThe measurement: 500 of 669 SCIP nodes are kind=\"local\" (74.7%); 498 of 1255\nSymbols share a stable_id, 493 of them locals; 2 validation violations, 0 after\na drop. The three options (drop / qualify / exclude-from-invariants) still frame\nthe choice, with option (a) now known ineffective for the stated purpose.\n\nThe kind-overload point also stands and is sharper than filed: symbol_kinds.py\ndefines `local` as \"Local symbol (Terraform local). CANONICAL per audit-findings\n0007\", and audits/0007 justifies canonicality because Terraform's locals block is\n\"a TOP-LEVEL Terraform construct\". Nothing sanctions the value for a function-body\nbinding. It has a live cost: selection/filters.py lists `local` in\nKEY_SYMBOL_KINDS under a \"# Terraform/HCL\" comment, so 447 of the 500 pass\nis_key_symbol and six of the artifact's top-14 highest-in-degree nodes are locals\nnamed '0'..'5', outranking almost every real function in the sketch budget.\n\n=== PROVENANCE ===\n\nThe `if scip_sym.is_local:` branch entered in ONE commit, 773000a11d (2026-04-19),\non the rationale \"preserved with kind='local' rather than dropped, because\nrust-analyzer and scip-python both emit locals that participate in cross-reference\nresolution ... meta carrying the raw SCIP symbol string for later linkers\".\ngit log -S finds NO commit modifying it since. The \"later linkers\" consumer does\nnot exist: the only reader of meta[\"scip_symbol\"] is translate.py's own\nintra-pass resolve map. Two commits have since edited _name_and_kind and both\nworked around the branch without questioning it -- one of them (ac4a481bf5, the\nINV-lagot _KIND_MAP fix) was mine, three lines away, earlier in this same session."  # eb69
